# `export-ignore` is `git archive`'s, and `git archive` is what `make tarball`
# builds a release with: everything listed here stays out of the tarball that
# `install.sh` and `flightdeck update` download.
#
# What stays behind is the repository's own machinery -- the CI definitions and
# the git dotfiles -- none of which means anything on a machine Flightdeck is
# installed on. The `tests/` and `docs/` directories are deliberately NOT here:
# a release somebody unpacks can be run against its own suite, and the docs are
# how they find out what it does.
#
# `docs/img/` IS here, though: 651 KB of pictures of the menu -- 360 KB of it
# the demo GIF, the rest the two stills -- taken to be looked at on
# GitHub, would be most of a release's download for somebody who is never going
# to open them. The markdown that links them still travels; on an unpacked
# release those three links point at nothing.
#
# `scripts/` is NOT here, and the reason is mechanical rather than a matter of
# taste: `tests/` travels so a release can be run against its own suite, and
# `tests/test_ansi2html.py` loads `scripts/ansi2html.py` by path. Excluding the
# directory would put a failing test in every release.
#
# Mind the reading order: `git archive` takes these rules from the `.gitattributes`
# in the COMMIT it is packing, not from the working tree. A change here only
# takes effect once it is committed.
# `.superpowers/` is a scratch directory some agent tooling creates; it is never
# tracked, and the rule is here so it cannot ship if a checkout ever tracks one.
.github         export-ignore
.gitignore      export-ignore
.gitattributes  export-ignore
.superpowers    export-ignore
docs/img        export-ignore
